  competing offers. With arithmetic, not platitudes.
- Weak answer versus strong answer, side by side, with the change named every time.
]

#note[
Everything in this chapter is a *structure*, not a script. Every filled example uses invented
people, invented projects and invented numbers. If you recite one of them, the interviewer will
hear it — these patterns are common enough that recruiters recognise borrowed lines instantly.
Take the slots. Fill them with your own real work and your own real offer letter.
]

// =====================================================================
#section("Part 1 — Group discussion")
// =====================================================================

#subsection("What a GD actually scores")

A GD is usually 8 to 12 candidates, one topic, 10 to 20 minutes, and one or two silent evaluators
with a grid. The grid does *not* have a row for "was right".

#table(columns: (auto, 1fr, auto),
  [*Row on the grid*], [*What earns the mark*], [*Rough weight*],
  [Content], [A point nobody else made, with a reason or an example], [high],
  [Structure], [Signposting, sequencing, summarising], [high],
  [Initiative], [Opening, or reviving a stalled discussion], [medium],
  [Team behaviour], [Bringing in a quiet person, handling a clash], [high],
  [Clarity], [Audible, unhurried, short sentences], [medium],
  [Listening], [Referring to what someone else said, by name], [medium],
  [Aggression / rudeness], [*Negative marks*. Interrupting, mocking, shouting], [fatal],
)

#trap[
*The three GD myths.*

*Myth 1: speak the most and you win.* False. Evaluators count *distinct contributions*, not
seconds. Four good entries beat fourteen repetitions.

*Myth 2: you must speak first.* False. Speaking first with a weak point is worse than speaking
third with a strong one. Speak first only if you have a real frame ready.

*Myth 3: you must "win" the argument.* False. A GD has no verdict. Candidates who try to defeat
others usually collect the aggression mark.
]

#formulas(title: "The realistic target")[
In a 15-minute GD with 10 people, aim for *4 to 6 entries of 25 to 40 seconds each*. That is about
2.5 minutes of speech. Any more and you are crowding; any less and the evaluator has no data.
]

#subsection("The four topic types and the frame for each")

#table(columns: (auto, 1fr, 1fr),
  [*Type*], [*Example shape*], [*Frame to open with*],
  [Factual], ["Should public transport be free?"], [Stakeholders: who pays, who benefits, who loses],
  [Abstract], ["A closed door"], [Define it two ways, then pick one and build],
  [Opinion], ["Is remote work good for freshers?"], [Split by context: for whom, in what role, at what stage],
  [Case], ["A retailer is losing customers. What now?"], [Diagnose, then options, then a recommendation],
)

#subsection("SPEH — the four slots for every entry")

#formulas(title: "SPEH")[
*S — Signpost.* Tell them what kind of thing you are about to say. "I want to add a second
stakeholder." / "I want to disagree with one part of that."

*P — Point.* One sentence. One idea.

*E — Evidence.* A reason, a number, a mechanism, or a concrete example. Never just repeat the point
louder.

*H — Handover.* Give the floor away deliberately. "Priya, you work part-time — does that match what
you have seen?"
]

The handover is the slot almost everyone skips, and it is the one that scores on the *team
behaviour* row.

#diagram(height: 3.6cm, caption: "One entry, 25 to 40 seconds. Four slots. The handover is what most candidates drop.")[
  #dnode(0pt, 12pt, 3.2cm, 1.0cm, "S\nSignpost\n\"I want to add...\"")
  #dnode(3.6cm, 12pt, 3.2cm, 1.0cm, "P\nPoint\none sentence")
  #dnode(7.2cm, 12pt, 3.2cm, 1.0cm, "E\nEvidence\nnumber or example")
  #dnode(10.8cm, 12pt, 3.2cm, 1.0cm, "H\nHandover\nname someone")
  #darrow(3.2cm, 30pt, 3.6cm, 30pt)
  #darrow(6.8cm, 30pt, 7.2cm, 30pt)
  #darrow(10.4cm, 30pt, 10.8cm, 30pt)
  #darrow(12.4cm, 52pt, 12.4cm, 70pt)
  #dnode(2.0cm, 70pt, 10.4cm, 0.7cm, "back to listening - do not speak again for at least 90 seconds")
]

#subsection("Weak entry vs strong entry, same topic")

Topic: *"Should companies require freshers to work from the office for the first year?"*

#trap[
*WEAK.* "Yeah I agree with what he said, I mean office is better, because you learn more and there
is more interaction and networking and all, and also work from home you get distracted, so yeah I
think office is better."
]

#sol[
*STRONG — with the four slots labelled.*

*S — Signpost:* "I want to sharpen Arjun's point rather than repeat it."

*P — Point:* "The benefit of the office for a fresher is not networking. It is *overheard
correction*."

*E — Evidence:* "In my internship I sat next to two senior developers. I learnt more from hearing
them argue about a database index for ten minutes than from any document. On a video call that
conversation would never have happened, because nobody schedules a meeting to think out loud. So
the real question is not office versus home, it is whether the team has a place where juniors can
overhear seniors reasoning."

*H — Handover:* "Sneha, you did a fully remote internship — did you get that anywhere, or did you
have to ask for everything explicitly?"
]

#formulas(title: "What exactly changed")[
1. The weak entry *agrees* and adds nothing. Evaluators mark that as a repetition, not a
   contribution.
2. It uses filler ("I mean", "and all", "so yeah") which costs the clarity mark.
3. The strong entry *reframes* the topic. "Not networking, but overheard correction" is a point
   nobody else in the room has.
4. The evidence is a *specific mechanism*, not a general belief.
5. It *ends by giving the floor away by name*. The next 30 seconds now belong to someone the
   evaluator saw you include.
6. Both are about 30 seconds. Length was never the difference.
]

#subsection("How to enter when nobody will stop talking")

#formulas(title: "The three-step entry")[
*1. Lean forward and raise a hand to chest height.* Not above your head. This is visible without
being theatrical, and evaluators see it even if the room does not.

*2. Use a bridge phrase on the first pause — half a second is enough.* Bridges that work:
- "Can I build on that?"
- "One quick addition —"
- "Two of us have said the same thing; may I offer the other side?"

*3. Say your point in the first 4 seconds.* Do not warm up. If you start with "I think that maybe
in some cases..." you will be talked over before the verb arrives.
]

#trap[
*Never say* "excuse me, excuse me, EXCUSE ME". Raising volume to enter is the fastest route to the
aggression mark. Raise your *hand*, then speak at normal volume into the gap.
]

#subsection("The four hard moments, and the words for each")

#table(columns: (auto, 1fr),
  [*Moment*], [*What to do*],
  [Someone talks over you mid-sentence],
  [Stop. Do not fight. When they finish: "I will finish the point I started — it was about X." You
   now look composed and they look rude.],
  [One person dominates the whole GD],
  [Do not attack them. Attack the *imbalance*: "We have heard three of us in detail. Meera and
   Karthik have not spoken — can we hear them before we go deeper?" This scores heavily on team
   behaviour.],
  [Someone states a wrong fact],
  [Correct the *claim*, never the person. "I would put that number differently — my understanding is
   closer to X. Either way, the direction of the argument holds." Softening lets them agree without
   losing face.],
  [The discussion has stalled or gone in circles],
  [Summarise and restart: "We have covered cost and convenience. We have not touched safety at all.
   Can we take that next?"],
)

#subsection("The summary — the highest-value 30 seconds in the room")

If the evaluator asks for a summary, or if the GD is running out of time, take it. A summary is the
single highest-scoring entry available.

#formulas(title: "The summary structure — four slots")[
*1. Points of agreement.* "We agreed on two things: ..."
*2. Points of disagreement, stated fairly.* "We split on whether ... Some argued X; others argued Y."
*3. What we missed.* "We did not discuss ..."
*4. A closing position.* "On balance the group leaned towards ..."
]

#sol[
*Filled example (topic: office attendance for freshers).*

"Let me pull the threads together. *We agreed* on two things: freshers need faster feedback than
seniors do, and a blanket rule for the whole company is a blunt instrument.

*We split* on the reason. Arjun and Sneha argued the benefit is social — belonging and networking.
Two others, including me, argued it is technical — overhearing how decisions get made.

*We did not discuss* cost at all, either the company's office cost or the fresher's commute and
rent, which in a metro can be a third of a first salary.

*On balance*, the group leaned towards 'office for the first six months, then the team decides' —
which is narrower than the question we were given, and I think that narrowing was the useful part
of this discussion."
]

#formulas(title: "Why that summary scores")[
1. It names *other people's* points accurately. That proves you were listening, which is its own
   grid row.
2. It states the disagreement *fairly*, including the side you were not on.
3. It adds *one new thing* — the cost angle — without starting a new argument.
4. It closes with a position, not a shrug.
5. Under 45 seconds.
]

#trap[
Do not summarise by saying "so basically everyone agreed that..." when they did not. A false
consensus is the most common summary failure and evaluators catch it instantly.
]

#subsection("Delivery: the small things that move the mark")

#table(columns: (auto, 1fr),
  [*Habit*], [*Fix*],
  [Filler words: "basically", "actually", "like", "I mean"],
  [Replace every filler with *silence*. A half-second pause reads as thinking. "Basically" reads as
   padding. Record yourself once and count them; most people are shocked.],
  [Speaking too fast, then trailing off],
  [Aim for about 130 words a minute; say the first sentence deliberately slowly and the rest follows
   that pace. Land the last three words of each sentence — most candidates fade exactly where the
   point is.],
  [Looking only at the evaluator],
  [Speak to the *group*. Sweep across faces. Evaluators specifically mark candidates who perform
   at them instead of participating.],
  [Notes],
  [If paper is allowed, write only names and one keyword each. It makes the handover slot easy.],
)

#trick[
Write the other candidates' names down in the first 30 seconds, in seating order. Using a person's
name is the cheapest way to score on listening and team behaviour, and almost nobody does it.
]

// =====================================================================
#section("Part 2 — Written communication")
// =====================================================================

Between rounds, almost all contact is by email or a messaging app. Recruiters forward these to
hiring managers. Every message is part of your evaluation.

#formulas(title: "The four rules for every message you send")[
*1. Subject line carries the fact.* "Interview reschedule request — Rahul Menon — 14 Oct slot".
Not "Regarding interview".

*2. First sentence says what you want.* Recruiters read on a phone between meetings.

*3. Under 150 words.* Always.

*4. One ask per message.* Two asks means one gets forgotten.
]

#subsection("Weak email vs strong email — the follow-up after silence")

#trap[
*WEAK.*

Subject: `Regarding my application`

`Respected Sir/Madam,`

`I am writing to you regarding the interview which I gave on the 3rd of this month for the role in
your esteemed organisation. It has been quite some time and I have not received any update
regarding the same. I am very much interested in this opportunity and I would be grateful if you
could kindly do the needful and let me know about my status at the earliest. Awaiting your positive
response. Kindly consider my request.`

`Thanking you,`
`Rahul`
]

#sol[
*STRONG.*

Subject: `Following up - Backend Engineer interview, 3 Oct - Rahul Menon`

`Hi Anita,`

`I interviewed for the Backend Engineer role on 3 October with Deepak and Sowmya. You mentioned a
decision by around the 14th, so I wanted to check in.`

`I am still very interested. One thing I did not get to mention: since the interview I finished
migrating my side project to a queue-based design, which is close to the batching problem Deepak
asked about. Happy to share the repository if useful.`

`I do have another process at a later stage, so any indication of timing would help me plan. No
pressure if the decision is not ready.`

`Thanks,`
`Rahul Menon · +91 98xxx xxxxx`
]

#formulas(title: "What exactly changed")[
1. The subject line *names the role, the date and the person*. It is searchable. "Regarding my
   application" is not.
2. It uses the recruiter's *name*, and names the interviewers. That proves you are a real person in
   their pipeline, not a mass mailer.
3. It *quotes the timeline they gave you*. This is a reminder without being a complaint.
4. It adds *new information* — a reason to reply beyond "are we there yet".
5. It mentions the other process *once, factually, without a deadline threat*. That is pressure done
   honestly.
6. It removes every hollow phrase: "esteemed organisation", "do the needful", "at the earliest",
   "kindly". These make you sound like a form letter.
7. 95 words instead of 110, and it says more.
]

#trap[
*Phrases to delete from your professional English, permanently:* "do the needful", "revert back",
"esteemed organisation", "kindly do the favour", "at the earliest", "Respected Sir/Madam", "I would
be highly obliged". They are not wrong English, but in a hiring inbox they read as a template, and
several sound like a demand dressed as politeness. Write "please", "reply", "let me know by Friday".
]

#subsection("Message structures for the other five moments")

Each is a *structure*. The filled example is invented. Replace every detail.

#formulas(title: "A. Thank-you note, sent within 24 hours")[
*Slot 1:* thank them, name the role and date.
*Slot 2:* one specific thing from the conversation that you found interesting — proof you were
present.
*Slot 3:* one sentence that closes a gap, if you fumbled something.
*Slot 4:* a short, non-pushy close.
]
#sol[
`Hi Deepak,`

`Thanks for the time this morning on the Backend Engineer role.`

`The part about moving retries out of the request path and into a queue stayed with me - I had not
thought about retry storms as a load problem rather than a correctness problem.`

`One correction: you asked how I would prevent duplicate charges, and I gave you a database-lock
answer. The cleaner answer is an idempotency key on the request, stored with the result, so a
repeat of the same key returns the stored result instead of charging again. I wanted to give you
the better answer rather than leave the weaker one standing.`

`Thanks again,`
`Rahul`
]
#note[
Slot 3 is powerful and almost nobody uses it. If you fumbled a question, a short written correction
turns a weak moment into evidence that you keep thinking. Keep it to *one* topic. Do not re-answer
the whole interview.
]

#formulas(title: "B. Asking to reschedule")[
*Slot 1:* the ask, in the first sentence.
*Slot 2:* a one-line reason. Honest and brief. Do not over-explain.
*Slot 3:* *three* concrete alternative slots with the time zone.
*Slot 4:* an apology for the inconvenience, once.
]
#sol[
`Hi Anita,`

`Could we move my interview from Thursday 14 Oct, 11:00 IST?`

`I have a university practical exam that was rescheduled onto the same morning.`

`I am free at any of these, all IST: Thu 14 Oct after 16:00, Fri 15 Oct 10:00-13:00, or Mon 18 Oct
any time. I can also do an earlier or later hour if that suits the panel better.`

`Sorry for the change, and thank you for accommodating it.`
]
#trap[
Never ask to reschedule with "can we do it some other day?" and no alternatives. You have just
handed the recruiter your work. Always give three slots, always with the time zone, especially for
a company in Singapore or Thailand where the offset differs from India by 1.5 hours.
]

#formulas(title: "C. Accepting an offer")[
*Slot 1:* accept clearly, in the first line. No hedging.
*Slot 2:* restate the three facts you are agreeing to: role, fixed pay, joining date.
*Slot 3:* ask what documents are needed next.
*Slot 4:* one line of genuine enthusiasm.
]
#sol[
`Hi Anita,`

`I am glad to accept the Backend Engineer offer.`

`Confirming my understanding: fixed pay of Rs 7,20,000 per year plus a variable component of
Rs 80,000, joining on 1 December 2026 at the Pune office.`

`Please let me know what you need from me - I have my degree certificate, the last three months of
payslips, and I can start the background verification this week.`

`Looking forward to working with Deepak's team.`
]
#trap[
*Always restate the numbers in writing.* Verbal offers get misremembered, and the number a recruiter
says on a call is often the *CTC*, not the fixed pay. Writing it down is not rude; it is how every
professional confirms an agreement. If the recruiter's reply corrects your numbers, you have just
caught a misunderstanding for free.
]

#formulas(title: "D. Declining an offer without burning the bridge")[
*Slot 1:* decline clearly, early, and thank them.
*Slot 2:* a short, true, non-comparative reason. You do not owe them the competitor's name or
salary.
*Slot 3:* something specific and positive about their process.
*Slot 4:* leave the door open, sincerely.
]
#sol[
`Hi Anita,`

`Thank you for the offer. After a lot of thought I am going to decline.`

`I have accepted a role that is closer to distributed systems work, which is the direction I want
to build in over the next few years. It was a genuinely hard decision.`

`I want to say that your process was the clearest I went through - Deepak's interview was the only
one where I learnt something, and the written feedback after round two was unusual and appreciated.`

`If there is a fit in the future I would be glad to talk again.`
]
#note[
You will meet these people again. India and Singapore tech hiring circles are small, and recruiters
move between companies constantly. A graceful decline is a real asset two years from now. A
disappeared candidate — someone who simply stops replying — is remembered, and it is remembered by
name.
]

#formulas(title: "E. Asking for more time to decide")[
*Slot 1:* thank them and say you want to accept properly, not quickly.
*Slot 2:* name a *specific* date, not "some more time".
*Slot 3:* a true reason at a high level.
*Slot 4:* confirm your continued interest.
]
#sol[
`Hi Anita,`

`Thank you for the offer - I am taking it seriously and want to give you a proper answer rather
than a rushed one.`

`Could I confirm by Friday 24 October? That is eight days from today.`

`I am at the final stage of one other process and expect to hear by the 22nd. I would rather decide
with complete information than accept and then reopen the conversation.`

`To be clear, I am genuinely interested in this role, and Pune works for me.`
]
#trap[
*Do not lie about a competing offer you do not have.* It is the most common piece of bad advice in
placement forums. Recruiters in the same city talk to each other, some ask for a written offer as
proof, and if you are caught the offer is withdrawn. Asking for a week to decide needs no excuse at
all — "I want to give you a proper answer" is a complete and honest reason.
]

// =====================================================================
#section("Part 3 — Questions to ask the interviewer")
// =====================================================================

At the end of almost every round you will hear "do you have any questions for us?" Saying "no, you
have covered everything" is a wasted opportunity and is often recorded as low interest.

#formulas(title: "Prepare six, ask two or three")[
Prepare six because three will get answered during the interview. Ask two or three. Then stop.
]

#table(columns: (auto, 1fr),
  [*Round*], [*What to ask*],
  [HR screen],
  [Team size and where the role sits. Joining date and location. What the first six months look
   like. Whether there is a bond or training agreement — ask this early, in writing if possible.],
  [Technical / hiring manager],
  [What the team is actually building this quarter. How code review and deployment work. The last
   hard technical decision the team made, and what they chose. What a fresher is expected to do
   alone by month six.],
  [Senior / leadership],
  [What would make this hire a success in a year. What the team is worst at today. Why the last
   person in this role left or moved.],
)

#subsection("Weak questions vs strong questions")

#trap[
*WEAK.*
- "What does your company do?" — you should already know.
- "How much leave do I get?" — ask HR after the offer, not the hiring manager before it.
- "Is there work-life balance here?" — nobody says no. You learn nothing.
- "What is the salary?" — not in round one; the process will reach it.
- "Do you provide training?" — reads as "I cannot learn by myself".
- "How soon can I get promoted?" — reads as "I am leaving soon".
]

#sol[
*STRONG.*
- "What is the team building this quarter, and where would a new engineer fit into that?"
- "What is the last technical decision the team argued about, and what did you choose?"
- "If someone joins in December, what would you want them doing on their own by June?"
- "What is the team worst at right now?"
- "Is the pay structure fixed plus variable, and how has the variable actually paid out in the last
  two years?"
- "Is there a service agreement or bond attached to this role? If so, what is the duration and the
  amount?"
]

#formulas(title: "What exactly changed")[
1. Weak questions ask about *benefits to you*. Strong ones ask about *the work*.
2. Every strong question has an answer that is *specific and checkable*. "Work-life balance?" gets a
   brochure answer; "what is the team worst at?" cannot be answered with a brochure.
3. The strong list includes two hard-money questions — variable pay history and bond — asked plainly
   and without apology. That is not rude. It is how you avoid a bad surprise in the offer letter.
4. The strong questions give *you* information you will actually use to decide.
]

#trick[
The single best question, and the one interviewers remember: *"what is the team worst at right
now?"* A good interviewer will answer honestly, and their answer tells you more about the job than
the entire job description. If they refuse to answer it at all, that is also information.
]

// =====================================================================
#section("Part 4 — Money: CTC, in-hand, and negotiation")
// =====================================================================

#subsection("First: CTC is not salary")

#formulas(title: "What sits inside an Indian CTC number")[
*CTC* (cost to company) = everything the company spends on you, including money you never see.

Typical components:
- *Fixed pay* — basic, HRA, allowances. This is the real salary.
- *Variable / performance pay* — paid only if targets are met. Often 10 to 20% of CTC.
- *Joining bonus* — paid once, often with a claw-back clause if you leave within a year.
- *Employer PF contribution* — goes to your provident fund, not your bank.
- *Gratuity provision* — you only receive it after (usually) five years of service.
- *Insurance premium* — the company pays an insurer, not you.

*Only fixed pay, minus your own PF and tax, reaches your bank account.*
]

#trap[
Two offers with the *same* CTC can differ by more than Rs 5,000 a month in take-home, depending on
how much of the CTC is variable and how the basic is structured. And an offer with a *lower* CTC
can pay you *more* every month. Never compare CTC. Compare monthly in-hand, and compare the fixed
component.
]

