Offer A  (headline CTC Rs 9.0 LPA)
  fixed pay                 Rs 6,57,500
  variable, NOT guaranteed  Rs 1,20,000
  your PF cut               Rs 35,505
  income tax + cess         Rs 0
  TAKE HOME per month       Rs 51,633

Offer B  (headline CTC Rs 7.8 LPA)
  fixed pay                 Rs 7,17,560
  variable, NOT guaranteed  Rs 0
  your PF cut               Rs 43,054
  income tax + cess         Rs 0
  TAKE HOME per month       Rs 56,009
```
]

#formulas(title: "Read that output again")[
Offer B has a CTC that is *Rs 1.2 lakh lower*, and pays *Rs 4,376 more every month*. That is
Rs 52,000 a year more in your bank.

The reason: Offer A packs Rs 1.2 lakh of its headline into variable pay that may or may not arrive,
plus a one-time joining bonus that stops after year one.

This is why "what is your CTC expectation?" is the wrong conversation. The right conversation is
*"what is the fixed component?"*
]

#note[
The tax ladder above is a simplified teaching shape, not a tax filing. Slabs, rebates and the
standard deduction change. Use your own offer letter's exact numbers and the current year's rules.
The *method* — strip out everything that is not fixed pay, then subtract PF and tax — does not
change.
]

#subsection("The salary question: what to actually say")

You will be asked "what are your salary expectations?" — often in the very first HR call.

#trap[
*WEAK answer 1.* "Whatever the company policy is, sir. I just want the experience."

You have told them you will accept the lowest number they have. You also sound like you have not
researched the role.
]

#trap[
*WEAK answer 2.* "I want 12 lakhs."

A single number with no basis. If it is too high you are screened out silently. If it is too low you
have capped yourself before they ever said a figure.
]

#sol[
*STRONG — the four-move structure.*

*Move 1 — deflect once, politely.* "I would rather understand the role fully first — the
responsibilities will shape what is fair. Do you have a band for this position?"

*Move 2 — if they push, give a researched range, not a point.* "Based on what I have seen for
backend roles at this experience level in Pune, the range looks like Rs 7 to 9 lakh fixed. I am
targeting that band."

*Move 3 — anchor on fixed, not CTC.* "To be clear, I am talking about fixed pay. I would want to
look at the variable component separately, since it is not guaranteed."

*Move 4 — close with flexibility that is real, not unlimited.* "The number matters, but so does
what I will be working on. If the role is strong I have some flexibility — though I do have a floor
I need to respect."
]

#formulas(title: "What exactly changed")[
1. The strong answer *asks for their band first*. In many companies the recruiter will simply tell
   you, and then you never have to name a number at all.
2. It gives a *range*, and the range has a *stated basis* ("backend roles, this level, this city").
   A number with a reason is negotiable; a number without one is a demand.
3. It says the word *fixed*. That one word reframes the whole negotiation, and most freshers never
   say it.
4. It signals flexibility *and a floor*. Unlimited flexibility is the same as having no number.
5. It never says "whatever you think is right".
]

#formulas(title: "How to build your range in 30 minutes")[
1. Ask three seniors from your own college, in the same role and city, what their *fixed* pay was.
   Ask for fixed, not CTC, or the data is useless.
2. Check two or three salary sites for the same role, level and city, and note that these skew high.
3. Take the *middle* of what you learn as your target, the bottom as your floor, and the middle plus
   20% as your ask.
4. Write all three numbers down before the call. Under pressure you will otherwise say the first
   number that comes into your head.
]

#trap[
*If you are asked for your current salary and you have one*, you may answer it — but in several
places asking is restricted, and you are always allowed to redirect: "My current fixed pay is
X. That was set when I joined two years ago, so I am basing my expectation on the market for this
role rather than on that number." Never inflate a current salary. Payslips are checked during
background verification, and a mismatch there ends the offer.
]

#subsection("Can a fresher negotiate at all?")

Often less than you hope, but more than zero. Know which door is open.

#table(columns: (auto, 1fr),
  [*Situation*], [*Realistic room*],
  [Mass campus hire, one common offer for 300 people],
  [*Almost none on salary.* The number is fixed by policy. Negotiate the *role*, *location* or
   *team* instead — those often have room and matter more over three years.],
  [Off-campus fresher hire, one-to-one process],
  [*5 to 15% is realistic*, mostly if you have a second offer or a rare skill.],
  [You hold a written competing offer],
  [*Real room.* This is the only lever that reliably moves a number.],
  [You have 1 to 3 years of experience],
  [*20 to 40% over current* is a normal jump in Indian tech. Anchor on market, not on your
   current pay.],
  [Offer already signed and accepted],
  [*None, and do not try.* Reopening a signed offer damages you and sometimes gets it withdrawn.],
)

#formulas(title: "The counter-offer structure — five slots")[
*Slot 1:* enthusiasm, genuine and specific. You want the job.
*Slot 2:* the gap, as a number, not a feeling.
*Slot 3:* the basis for your number.
*Slot 4:* an alternative if the base cannot move — joining bonus, earlier review, relocation
support, a better title, a shorter bond.
*Slot 5:* a clear commitment: if they meet it, you accept.
]

#sol[
*Filled example — invented numbers, replace with yours.*

"Anita, thank you — I want to say first that I want this role. Deepak's team is doing the work I
want to do.

I wanted to talk about the fixed component. The offer is Rs 6.5 lakh fixed. Based on what I have
seen for backend roles at this level in Pune, and on the other written offer I am holding, I was
targeting closer to Rs 7.5 lakh.

If the base genuinely cannot move, there are other ways to close the gap that would work for me: a
joining bonus, a review at six months instead of twelve, or relocation support.

If we can get to Rs 7.2 lakh fixed, I will accept today and stop my other process."
]

#formulas(title: "Why that works")[
1. It *opens and closes with commitment*. A negotiation that does not end in "then I accept" is just
   a complaint.
2. It names *one* number and *one* gap. Negotiating five things at once fails.
3. It offers *alternative currencies*. Base pay is often locked by band; a joining bonus usually is
   not, and an early review costs the company nothing today.
4. It gives a *precise close*: Rs 7.2 lakh, today, and the other process stops. The recruiter can now
   take a concrete proposal to their manager.
5. It is *honest*. It mentions a real competing offer. If you do not have one, delete that clause —
   the rest of the structure still works.
]

#trap[
*Never negotiate over a chat message or in a rush.* Ask for a 10-minute call. Text negotiations get
forwarded, lose tone and get misread. And never give an ultimatum you are not willing to follow
through on — recruiters call bluffs more often than students expect.
]

// =====================================================================
#section("Part 5 — Bond and service agreements")
// =====================================================================

Many Indian service companies, and some product companies, attach a *service agreement* (often
called a bond) to a fresher offer: stay N months or pay a penalty.

#formulas(title: "The six questions to ask before you sign")[
1. *Duration.* How many months, and does it start from joining or from the end of training?
2. *Amount.* The exact rupee figure, and is it reduced pro-rata as you serve?
3. *Trigger.* Does it apply only if you resign, or also if the company releases you?
4. *Documents.* Do they hold your original degree certificate or marksheets?
5. *Relieving letter.* Is it withheld if you leave early?
6. *Transfer.* Does an internal move or a location change reset the clock?
]

#trap[
*The one that actually hurts is not the money.* It is question 5. A withheld *relieving letter* or a
withheld original certificate can block your next job entirely, because background verification
requires them. A candidate who paid the penalty but cannot get a relieving letter is in a far worse
position than one who paid nothing and left cleanly. Ask about the relieving letter in writing
before you sign, and keep the reply.
]

#subsection("How to price a bond")

Spread the penalty over the lock-in. That converts a scary lump sum into a monthly number you can
compare against the offer.

#code(lang: "js", caption: "bond.js — the monthly price of your own flexibility")[
```js
function bondCost(penalty, months) { return penalty / months; }

for (const [p, m] of [[200000, 24], [200000, 12], [100000, 18], [50000, 12]]) {
  console.log('Rs ' + p.toLocaleString('en-IN') + ' over ' + m + ' months  ->  Rs ' +
    Math.round(bondCost(p, m)).toLocaleString('en-IN') + ' per month of lost freedom');
}
```
]
#code(lang: "text", caption: "output")[
```text
Rs 2,00,000 over 24 months  ->  Rs 8,333 per month of lost freedom
Rs 2,00,000 over 12 months  ->  Rs 16,667 per month of lost freedom
Rs 1,00,000 over 18 months  ->  Rs 5,556 per month of lost freedom
Rs 50,000 over 12 months  ->  Rs 4,167 per month of lost freedom
```
]

#note[
Read the first two lines together. The *same* Rs 2 lakh penalty is twice as expensive per month when
the bond is short — because you are paying to escape a shorter remaining commitment. So when you are
comparing two bonded offers, a longer bond with the same penalty is *less* costly per month, but it
locks you for longer. Decide which of the two you care about, and say so out loud to yourself before
signing.
]

#subsection("Asking about the bond without sounding like you plan to leave")

#trap[
*WEAK.* "What happens if I want to leave before two years?"

The interviewer now believes you intend to leave.
]

#sol[
*STRONG.* "I want to be sure I understand the full agreement before I commit to it, so a few
straightforward questions: is there a service agreement attached, how long is it, and is it a fixed
amount or reduced pro-rata as I serve? And are any original documents held during that period?"
]

#formulas(title: "What exactly changed")[
1. The strong version frames the question as *diligence before committing*, which is exactly what it
   is, rather than as an exit plan.
2. It asks *all* the questions in one go, so you sound prepared rather than nervous.
3. It uses the correct term, *service agreement*, which signals you have read a contract before.
4. It slips in the document question last, quietly, and that is the answer that matters most.
]

// =====================================================================
#section("Part 6 — Notice period and joining date")
// =====================================================================

If you already have a job, the notice period drives everything. Work *backwards* from the date the
new company wants you.

#code(lang: "js", caption: "notice.js — backward planning from the joining date")[
```js
function addDays(d, n) { const x = new Date(d); x.setDate(x.getDate() + n); return x; }
function fmt(d) { return d.toISOString().slice(0, 10); }

function plan({ joinBy, noticeDays, handoverBuffer = 5, bgvDays = 10 }) {
  const join = new Date(joinBy);
  const lastWorkingDay = addDays(join, -1);
  const resignBy = addDays(lastWorkingDay, -noticeDays);
  return {
    join: fmt(join),
    lastWorkingDay: fmt(lastWorkingDay),
    resignBy: fmt(resignBy),
    safeResignBy: fmt(addDays(resignBy, -handoverBuffer)),
    tellNewCompanyEarliestJoin: fmt(addDays(new Date(), noticeDays + handoverBuffer)),
    offerLetterExpected: fmt(addDays(new Date(), bgvDays)),
  };
}

console.log('today is treated as', fmt(new Date()));
for (const [k, v] of Object.entries(plan({ joinBy: '2026-12-01', noticeDays: 60 }))) {
  console.log('  ' + k.padEnd(28) + v);
}

console.log('\nIf notice is 90 days instead of 60:');
const q = plan({ joinBy: '2026-12-01', noticeDays: 90 });
console.log('  you must resign by         ' + q.resignBy);
console.log('  safe date with handover    ' + q.safeResignBy);

function buyout(monthlyBasic, daysToBuy) { return Math.round(monthlyBasic / 30 * daysToBuy); }
console.log('\nBuying out 30 days on a Rs 30,000 monthly basic costs about Rs',
  buyout(30000, 30).toLocaleString('en-IN'));
```
]
#code(lang: "text", caption: "output (run on 15 September 2026)")[
```text
today is treated as 2026-09-15
  join                        2026-12-01
  lastWorkingDay              2026-11-30
  resignBy                    2026-10-01
  safeResignBy                2026-09-26
  tellNewCompanyEarliestJoin  2026-11-19
  offerLetterExpected         2026-09-25

If notice is 90 days instead of 60:
  you must resign by         2026-09-01
  safe date with handover    2026-08-27

Buying out 30 days on a Rs 30,000 monthly basic costs about Rs 30,000
```
]

#formulas(title: "Read the 90-day block carefully")[
It says you should have resigned on *1 September* — two weeks in the past. The script is telling you
something true and uncomfortable: with a 90-day notice you *cannot* make a 1 December joining date.

So do not promise it. Your options are:
1. Ask the new company for a January joining date, now, before you accept.
2. Ask your current employer for a partial waiver of notice. Many will grant 15 to 30 days.
3. Buy out the remaining days, if your contract allows it. The last line prices that.

All three are honest. Promising a date you cannot meet is not, and it is the single most common way
freshers and early-career candidates lose an offer *after* signing it.
]

#subsection("The notice-period question in the interview")

#trap[
*WEAK.* "Notice period is 90 days but I will try to manage it, maybe I can come in 30 days."

"I will try to manage" is a promise with no mechanism. The recruiter writes down 30 days, plans
around it, and when you cannot deliver, trust is gone.
]

#sol[
*STRONG.* "My notice period is 90 days from the date I resign. Realistically that puts my earliest
joining date at around 20 December if I resign the day I sign.

There are two ways it could be shorter. My contract allows a buy-out of up to 30 days at basic pay,
which would cost me about Rs 30,000 and bring it to late November. And my manager has released two
people early in the past, though I cannot promise that.

So my honest answer is: 20 December is the date I can commit to. If either of those works out I
will tell you immediately and join sooner."
]

#formulas(title: "What exactly changed")[
1. The strong version gives *one committable date* and treats everything else as upside.
2. It names the *mechanisms* — buy-out clause, manager discretion — with the cost of each.
3. It separates what it *can promise* from what it *cannot*, explicitly.
4. It commits to *telling them early* if the date improves. That is the sentence a recruiter
   remembers.
5. It never uses the words "I will try to manage".
]

#trap[
*Do not resign before you have the written offer letter in hand and background verification is
clear.* A verbal offer is not an offer. This is the most expensive mistake in this chapter: people
resign on a phone call, the written offer is delayed or withdrawn, and they are left with neither
job.
]

// =====================================================================
#section("Part 7 — Working across borders")
// =====================================================================

If you are interviewing with a company in Singapore, Thailand, Indonesia or Vietnam, three extra
things get scored that never come up in a domestic interview: whether you have thought about the
*work pass*, whether you have thought about the *real cost of living*, and whether your
*communication style* will work in that office.

#subsection("The work pass conversation — raise it first")

#trap[
*WEAK.* Wait for them to bring it up, then say "I think the company will handle it."

The company does handle the filing. But the *timeline* and the *eligibility* affect your joining
date, and a candidate who has not thought about it looks like a candidate who will be a surprise
in month two.
]

#sol[
*STRONG.* "Before we go further I want to check the pass situation, because it affects the date I
can commit to. My understanding is that for this kind of role the company sponsors the application,
and the processing takes a few weeks after the offer is signed, sometimes longer. Two questions: is
there a minimum salary threshold for the pass type you would apply for, and does your offer process
assume a joining date after approval or before it?"
]

#formulas(title: "What exactly changed, and what to actually check")[
The strong version raises it *early*, treats it as a *shared planning problem*, and asks the two
questions that actually change your life: the salary threshold and whether the joining date assumes
approval.

*Check these yourself before any SE Asia interview, on the official government site, because the
numbers change every year:*
1. Which pass type applies to your role and experience level.
2. The *current* minimum qualifying salary for that pass, which usually rises with age and
   experience.
3. Whether the pass is tied to the employer (it almost always is), and what happens if you resign.
4. Typical processing time, and whether you may enter the country before approval.
5. Dependant rules, if that matters to you.

Never quote a threshold from a forum post or from this book. Quote the official figure you looked
up this month.
]

#subsection("Comparing an Indian offer with a Singapore offer")

You cannot compare the headline numbers. The currencies, the tax systems, the retirement
contributions and the rent are all different.

#formulas(title: "The six-line comparison — do it on paper before you decide")[
1. *Annualise both.* Is the SGD figure monthly? Is there a 13th month payment? Is the Indian figure
   CTC or fixed?
2. *Strip to fixed pay* on both sides. Remove variable, bonuses and employer retirement
   contributions.
3. *Subtract retirement contributions* — CPF in Singapore, PF in India. These are real savings, but
   they are not spendable income, so keep them in a separate line.
4. *Subtract income tax* using each country's own rates. They are not comparable rates.
5. *Subtract rent and daily living.* This is where the comparison usually turns.
6. *Compare what is left*, converted at today's rate, and separately compare the *savings* line.
]

#ex(1, tier: 2, asked: "Grab · pattern")[
Offer A: Singapore, SGD 5,000 per month fixed, 12 months, no bonus.
Offer B: Bengaluru, Rs 14,00,000 CTC of which Rs 2,00,000 is variable.
Which one leaves you with more spendable money, and which leaves you with more savings?
Use SGD 1 = Rs 65 and show every step.
]
#sol[
*Step 1 — annualise.*
- A: 5,000 $times$ 12 $=$ SGD 60,000 per year.
- B: CTC 14,00,000. Strip the variable: fixed is about Rs 12,00,000. Strip employer PF and gratuity,
  roughly Rs 80,000 together, leaving about *Rs 11,20,000* of real fixed pay.

*Step 2 — retirement contributions.* Assume the Singapore employee contribution is around 20% of
the monthly wage up to a ceiling, and the Indian employee PF is 12% of basic.
- A: employee contribution roughly SGD 12,000 a year goes to CPF. Spendable base: SGD 48,000.
- B: employee PF roughly Rs 60,000 a year. Spendable base: Rs 10,60,000.

*Step 3 — tax.* Singapore's resident rates on this income are low, a few percent effective. India's
effective rate on Rs 11 lakh is higher. Take rough effective rates of 3% and 7%.
- A: 48,000 $times$ 0.97 $approx$ SGD 46,600.
- B: 10,60,000 $times$ 0.93 $approx$ Rs 9,86,000.

*Step 4 — rent and living.* This is the step people skip and it decides the answer.
- A: a room in a shared flat, plus transport and food, realistically SGD 1,800 to 2,500 a month.
  Use SGD 2,200: that is SGD 26,400 a year.
- B: a 1BHK plus transport and food in Bengaluru, realistically Rs 35,000 a month.
  That is Rs 4,20,000 a year.

*Step 5 — what is left.*
- A: 46,600 $-$ 26,400 $=$ SGD 20,200 $=$ Rs 13,13,000 at Rs 65.
- B: 9,86,000 $-$ 4,20,000 $=$ Rs 5,66,000.

*Step 6 — the savings line, kept separate.*
- A: CPF about SGD 12,000 plus an employer share on top $=$ Rs 7.8 lakh or more, but *locked* and
  largely not withdrawable if you leave Singapore permanently before retirement age.
- B: PF about Rs 60,000 plus an employer share, withdrawable under Indian rules.

*Answer.* Offer A leaves roughly *2.3 times* more spendable money per year at these assumptions.
But the savings in A are locked in a foreign retirement system and the pass is tied to the employer,
so the risk profile is different. Both facts belong in the decision.
]
#ans[A, by a wide margin on spendable income — but say the CPF lock-in and the employer-tied pass out loud]

#trap[
Every number in that example is an assumption, including the exchange rate, the tax rates, the CPF
rates and the rent. Redo it with *your* offer letter, *this* month's rates, and rent quotes from a
real listings site for the district you would actually live in. The *method* is the transferable
part; the numbers are not.
]

#subsection("Why do you want to move to South East Asia?")

This is asked in almost every Tier 2 interview, and it is a screen for whether you will leave after
eight months.

#trap[
*WEAK.* "Singapore is a developed country with better opportunities and exposure. I want to grow my
career and experience the international work culture."

Nothing here is about the company, the product, or the work. It also applies equally to eleven other
countries, which tells the interviewer you are optimising for "abroad", not for this job.
]

#sol[
*STRONG.* "Two reasons, one about the work and one about me.

The work: the products in this region run across six or seven markets at once, with different
languages, currencies, and payment rails in each. I have never built anything that had to handle
that, and I think it is a genuinely different engineering problem from building for one market at
scale. The last company I interviewed with in India was solving depth; you are solving breadth.

About me: I have an aunt's family in Singapore and I have visited twice, so I am not romanticising
it. I know the food is expensive, I know a room in a shared flat is what I could afford in year
one, and I know the pass is tied to the employer. I would rather say that plainly than discover it
in month three."
]

#formulas(title: "What exactly changed")[
1. It names a *technical* reason specific to the region — multi-market systems — not a lifestyle
   reason.
2. It contrasts with the alternative ("depth versus breadth"), which shows a real comparison was
   made.
3. It *removes the naivety risk*, which is the interviewer's actual worry. Saying "I know rent is
   high and the pass is tied to you" is the single most reassuring thing a foreign candidate can
   say.
4. It is specific and personal without being a sob story.
]

#note[
As always, substitute your own reason. If you have never visited, say so honestly and replace that
second half with what you *have* done: read about the cost of living, spoken to two seniors who
moved, checked the pass rules. Honest preparation reads as well as personal experience. Inventing a
relative who lives there does not.
]

#subsection("Communication style — what actually differs")

#table(columns: (auto, 1fr, 1fr),
  [*Situation*], [*Common Indian-office habit*], [*What travels better regionally*],
  [Disagreeing with a senior],
  [Soften so much the disagreement disappears; say "yes sir" and then not do it],
  [State the disagreement plainly, once, with a reason, then commit to whatever is decided],
  [Saying you cannot meet a date],
  ["I will try", "we will manage"],
  ["I cannot make the 14th. I can make the 21st. Here is why."],
  [Status updates],
  [Report only when finished, or when it is already too late],
  [Short, regular, boring updates, including "no progress this week, blocked on X"],
  [Asking for help],
  [Wait, in case asking looks weak],
  [Ask after a stated time-box: "I gave this two hours, here is what I tried, here is where I am"],
  [Email register],
  [Very formal, long, deferential],
  [Short, direct, first-name, no honorifics unless the office uses them],
  [Meetings],
  [Silence unless called on],
  [One prepared point per meeting, said early],
)

#trap[
*The "I will try" problem.* In many regional offices, "I will try" is heard as "yes". So when the
date is missed, the other person believes they were misled, even though you thought you had hedged.
Replace *every* "I will try" with either a commitment or a refusal plus an alternative. This is the
single highest-value communication change for an Indian engineer moving into a regional team, and
it is also the reason the notice-period answer in Part 6 avoided that phrase.
]

#trap[
*Do not confuse directness with rudeness.* Direct means "I disagree with the caching approach,
here is why". It does not mean "that is a bad idea". The point stays about the work, never about the
person — which is the same rule as the GD in Part 1.
]

// =====================================================================
#section("Part 8 — A ladder of worked interview moments")
// =====================================================================

Same skills, three levels of pressure. Read them in order — the Tier 3 answers are only reachable
because of the habits built in Tier 1.

#tier-header(1)

#ex(2, tier: 1, asked: "TCS NQT · pattern")[
"Are you willing to relocate anywhere in India?"
]
#sol[
*The trap.* Both extremes lose. "Anywhere, sir!" when it is not true means you will decline the
posting later and burn the relationship. "Only my home city" at a company that posts freshers by
allocation usually ends the process.

*The structure — three slots.*
*Slot 1 — a clear yes or a clear no.*
*Slot 2 — one honest constraint, if you have one.*
*Slot 3 — what you have already done to prepare, so it is not just words.*

*Filled example — substitute your own truth.*
"Yes, I am willing to relocate. I have lived away from home for my whole degree, so I know what it
costs and I am comfortable with it.

One thing I should say honestly: my father has a medical condition and I visit every two months. So
I would want to be somewhere with reasonable connectivity back to Nagpur. That rules out very few
places.

I have already looked at accommodation in Pune and Chennai because those came up as likely
locations, so I am not saying yes in the abstract."
]
#ans[A clear yes, one real constraint, and evidence of preparation]
#note[
If you truly cannot relocate, say so early and directly. Losing a process in week one costs you a
week. Accepting a posting you cannot take and backing out in month three costs you the
relationship and sometimes your campus's relationship with that company.
]

#note[
*Backlogs, study gaps, a low CGPA and rejection history* are handled in full in Chapter 6. The
pattern is the same three-sentence shape used above: the fact owned without blame, what changed and
when, then the evidence that it stuck — and then stop talking. Never blame the university, a
teacher or bad luck, and never invent a hardship that did not happen.
]

#tier-header(2)

#ex(3, tier: 2, asked: "Shopee · pattern")[
"Your English is good but your accent is quite different from the rest of the team, who are mostly
based in Singapore and Jakarta. How will you handle that?"
]
#sol[
*Why this is asked.* It is not a test of your accent. It is a test of whether you get defensive when
someone names a real friction.

*The structure.*
*Slot 1 — accept the observation without apologising for who you are.*
*Slot 2 — name the actual mechanism that solves it.*
*Slot 3 — a concrete habit you already use.*

*Filled example — substitute your own.*
"That is fair, and it is a real thing on calls, not an imagined one.

What I have found matters more than accent is *pace and structure*. When I slow down and say the
conclusion first — 'the job failed, here is why, here is what I need' — people follow me easily. When
I speak fast and build up to the point, they do not.

Two things I already do: I write the one-line summary in the chat before I say it out loud on a
call, so there is a text version people can re-read, and I ask 'does that make sense so far?' once
in the middle rather than only at the end. If someone needs me to repeat something I would rather
they say so, and I will ask the team directly to tell me when that happens."
]
#ans[Accept, then redirect to pace and structure, then give two concrete habits]

#tier-header(3)

#ex(4, tier: 3, asked: "Amazon · pattern")[
"Tell me about a time you disagreed with a decision, lost the argument, and then had to make the
decision succeed anyway." Then expect three follow-up probes.
]
#sol[
*Why this is asked.* It separates people who are merely opinionated from people who can disagree
*and then commit*. The follow-ups are where most candidates fall apart, so prepare for them.

*The answer, four STAR parts labelled — substitute your own experience.*

*S — Situation.* "During my internship, my team was deciding how to store audit records for a
payments service. I argued for a separate append-only table. The tech lead chose to reuse the
existing orders table with a status column, because it was two days of work instead of two weeks and
we had a compliance deadline."

*T — Task.* "I was assigned to build it his way. I still believed it was the wrong long-term choice,
and I was the one who would maintain it for the rest of my internship."

*A — Action.* "I said my objection once more, in writing, in the design document, in two lines — so
it was on record without being re-litigated in every stand-up. Then I stopped arguing and built it
properly. I did three things to reduce the risk I was worried about: I put every audit write behind
one function so a future migration would touch one file instead of forty; I added a test that fails
if anyone writes an audit row outside that function; and I wrote a one-page note on what the
migration would look like if the table ever became a problem, with an estimate."

*R — Result.* "We shipped four days before the compliance deadline. Seven months later, after I had
left, the team did split the table out — and the lead messaged me to say the migration took two days
instead of the two weeks he had estimated, because of the single write path. He also said my
objection note was what made them look at it at all."

*Follow-up probe 1: "Why did you stop arguing?"*
"Because the decision was made by the person accountable for it, with information I did not have —
he knew the compliance deadline was immovable and I did not. Continuing to argue would have cost
team time and changed nothing. Disagreeing once clearly is useful; disagreeing repeatedly after a
decision is just noise."

*Follow-up probe 2: "What if you had been right and it had failed badly?"*
"Then the single write path and the migration note were exactly the insurance for that case. I did
not just comply — I reduced the cost of being wrong. That is what I would do again."

*Follow-up probe 3: "What would you do differently?"*
"I would have asked *why* the deadline was immovable before arguing, not after. I spent twenty
minutes arguing from a position that assumed we had time. If I had asked first, I would have argued
for the same design but with a smaller scope, which might actually have won."
]
#ans[Disagree once, in writing, then commit fully — and reduce the cost of being wrong]

#formulas(title: "Why the follow-ups matter more than the story")[
Tier 3 interviewers almost never accept the first answer. They probe three times, and the probes
follow a predictable shape:
1. *"Why?"* — they are testing whether your reasoning is real or rehearsed.
2. *"What if it had gone the other way?"* — they are testing whether you thought about risk.
3. *"What would you do differently?"* — they are testing self-awareness, and a candidate who says
   "nothing" fails here almost automatically.

Prepare all three for every story you plan to tell. If you cannot answer the third one honestly,
the story is not ready.
]

#ex(5, tier: 3, asked: "Goldman Sachs · pattern")[
The hiring manager says: "We can do Rs 16 lakh fixed. I know you mentioned 18. That is our band and
I cannot go above it." You do want the job. What do you say?
]
#sol[
*The wrong instincts.* Accept immediately (you leave value and look like you invented the 18), or
repeat 18 (you have added nothing new, so you will get the same answer).

*The structure — four moves.*
*Move 1 — accept the constraint out loud.* This is not surrender; it stops you arguing against a
wall.
*Move 2 — move to a different currency.* Base is banded. Other things are not.
*Move 3 — make one specific, small, concrete ask.*
*Move 4 — commit.*

*Filled example — substitute your own numbers.*
"Understood, and thank you for being straight about the band rather than leaving me guessing.

If 16 is the ceiling on base, let me ask about the things outside it. Three options, and any one of
them closes this for me:

First, a joining bonus of Rs 1.5 lakh. That is one-time and usually sits outside the band.

Second, a formal compensation review at six months instead of twelve, written into the offer. That
costs nothing today.

Third, if neither is possible, I would ask for the title to be Engineer II rather than Engineer I,
if the work justifies it — because that changes my band at the *next* review, which is worth more to
me over three years than the Rs 2 lakh anyway.

If any one of those works, I will sign today and stop my other process."
]
#ans[Accept the band, then negotiate joining bonus, review timing, or level — and close]

#formulas(title: "Why this works when repeating the number does not")[
1. *Move 1 removes the fight.* The manager is now not defending the band, because you agreed with
   it.
2. *Move 2 is the actual insight:* base pay is set by a compensation band that a hiring manager
   genuinely cannot break. Joining bonus, review date and level are usually *different approval
   paths*, and often easier.
3. *Move 3 gives three options, not one demand.* The manager gets to choose, which makes saying yes
   easy.
4. *The third option is the smartest one* — level compounds over years, and Rs 2 lakh once does not.
5. *Move 4 makes it a deal, not a complaint.*
6. If all three are refused, you have lost nothing. You accept or decline on the same terms you
   already had, and you did it professionally.
]

#trap[
Everything above assumes the 18 figure was *real* — that you researched it and you meant it. If you
invented it as an opening bluff, this conversation goes badly, because the follow-up is always
"where did 18 come from?" and you will have no answer. Research first, then negotiate.
]

// =====================================================================
#section("Part 9 — Closing behavioural questions, in STAR")
// =====================================================================

The last round often circles back to behaviour, especially around teams and conflict — which is
exactly what a GD is meant to predict.

#subsection("Q: Tell me about a time you disagreed with your team.")

#trap[
*WEAK.* "In my group project one guy wasn't doing anything so I just did his part myself and we
submitted on time. I don't like conflict so I avoided it."
]

#sol[
*STRONG — four STAR parts labelled.*

*S — Situation.* "In my seventh-semester project, four of us were building a campus complaint
portal. Two weeks before submission, our team lead wanted to rewrite the whole front end in a
framework none of us had used, because he thought it would look better to the panel."

*T — Task.* "I thought the rewrite would cost us the deadline, and I was the one who would have to
reconnect all the API calls. I had to either raise it or go along with it."

*A — Action.* "I did not argue in the group chat. I asked for fifteen minutes with him in person.
I said his goal was right — the front end did look dated — and then I put a number on the risk: I
counted 23 API calls and 9 forms that would need rewiring, and estimated three full days minimum
with nobody available to test. I proposed a smaller version: keep the structure, restyle the four
screens the panel would actually see, and put the framework rewrite in our 'future work' slide,
which the panel liked anyway. I offered to do the restyle myself over the weekend so he was not
paying for my objection with his own time."

*R — Result.* "He agreed the same evening. The restyle took me about eleven hours. We submitted two
days early, and the 'future work' slide became a question in the viva, which we answered well. He
and I worked together again on our final-year project, which I think matters more than the
disagreement itself."
]

#formulas(title: "What exactly changed")[
1. The weak answer has *no disagreement in it at all*. It describes avoidance, then says "I don't
   like conflict" — which answers the opposite of the question.
2. The strong *S* gives a real, specific technical disagreement with stakes.
3. The *T* says clearly what was at risk *for the speaker*, not just for the team.
4. The *A* shows four separate skills: choosing a private channel, agreeing with the other person's
   goal first, quantifying the risk (23 calls, 9 forms, 3 days), and offering a compromise that
   costs the speaker something.
5. The *R* gives measurable outcomes *and* a relationship outcome. The last line is what separates
   a good answer from a great one: it proves the disagreement did not damage the team.
6. Length: about 50 seconds. Weak answer: 12 seconds. The gap is content, not padding.
]

#note[
Say it again: *this is not your story.* The project, the framework, the 23 API calls — all invented
to show the shape. Open a notebook, list three real disagreements from your own projects,
internships or part-time work, and write each one into the four slots. If a slot is empty, that
story is not ready to use yet.
]

#subsection("Q: Why should we hire you, as the last question of the day?")

#trap[
*WEAK.* "Because I am a hard worker, a quick learner, and a good team player. I will give my 100%
to this company."

Every candidate that day said this. It contains no information.
]

#sol[
*STRONG — three slots: match, proof, gap.*

*Match.* "You said the team's biggest problem this quarter is that the ingestion jobs fail silently
and someone finds out the next morning."

*Proof.* "That is close to what I spent my internship on. I added structured logging and a
dead-letter queue to a batch job that was dropping about 2% of records without anyone noticing, and
wrote the alert that caught it. I can show you the code.

*Gap, stated honestly.* "What I do not have is production experience at your scale. I have never
run anything with more than a few thousand records an hour. I would need someone to review my work
closely for the first couple of months, and I would rather say that now than discover it in month
two."
]

#formulas(title: "What exactly changed")[
1. The strong answer *quotes the interviewer's own words back*, which proves you listened for the
   whole hour.
2. It gives *one* piece of proof with a number and an offer to show the code.
3. It *names a real gap*. This is counter-intuitive and it works: admitting a limit makes
   everything else you claimed more believable. It also protects you — a candidate who oversells
   then struggles in month two is remembered badly.
4. It says nothing about being a hard worker, a quick learner, or giving 100%.
]

#trap[
*Honest framing, never invention.* If you have a backlog, a study gap, a low CGPA or a rejection
history, say it plainly, in one sentence, and then spend the rest of the answer on *evidence of
improvement* — the cleared backlog, the rising semester scores, the projects built during the gap.
Never invent an experience you did not have. Background verification, a probing follow-up question,
or a simple "show me the code" will expose it, and a discovered lie ends the process at any stage,
including after joining.
]

// =====================================================================
#section("Part 9b — A mock group discussion, scored line by line")
// =====================================================================

Read this once for the flow, then read the score column. Six candidates, topic:
*"Should companies be allowed to monitor employees' work laptops?"* Twelve minutes.
All names and lines are invented.

#table(columns: (auto, 1fr, auto),
  [*Time*], [*What was said*], [*Evaluator note*],

  [0:10],
  [*Ravi:* "I'll start. I think we should first agree what 'monitoring' means, because keystroke
   logging and counting how many hours a laptop is on are very different things. I'd suggest we take
   them separately. Let me start with the milder one."],
  [*Strong open.* He did not just claim the first slot — he *defined the term* and *proposed a
   structure*. Initiative and Structure both.],

  [0:50],
  [*Meena:* "I agree with Ravi. Monitoring is important for security. Companies have data to
   protect."],
  [*Weak.* Agreement plus a generic claim. No evidence, no new angle. This counts as
   participation but not contribution.],

  [1:10],
  [*Karthik:* "Yeah exactly, and also productivity. If people are not working the company should
   know. That is basic." (He goes on to repeat this same point three more times before minute six.)],
  [*Weak, and slightly worse.* "That is basic" dismisses future disagreement. The repetitions are
   logged as repetitions, not as contributions. Small negative on team behaviour.],

  [1:35],
  [*Sneha:* "Can I take the other side on the productivity point specifically? The problem with
   measuring activity is that it measures the wrong thing. If I am stuck on a bug and thinking, my
   laptop looks idle. If I am mindlessly clicking through tickets, it looks productive. So activity
   monitoring does not just fail to measure output — it actively rewards the wrong behaviour."],
  [*Very strong.* A signposted, narrowly scoped disagreement with a *mechanism*, not an opinion.
   This is the best entry of the discussion so far.],

  [2:20],
  [*Arjun:* (talks over Sneha's last words) "But companies pay salaries so they have the right to
   check the time they paid for." — *Sneha:* (stops, waits, then) "I'll let Arjun finish and come
   back to the measurement point."],
  [*Negative for Arjun:* interrupting, and a position with no support. *Strong recovery for Sneha:*
   she did not fight for the floor. Composure is visible and it is marked.],

  [3:05],
  [*Ravi:* "Let me connect Arjun and Sneha. Arjun's point is about *rights* — can they. Sneha's is
   about *effectiveness* — does it work. Those are different questions and I think we are mixing
   them. Can we finish 'does it work' first, since that is where we actually disagree?"],
  [*Excellent.* He separated two conflated arguments and proposed a sequence. This is the single
   highest-scoring move available in a GD.],

  [4:00],
  [*Priya:* (first time speaking) "On whether it works — there is a second effect nobody has
   mentioned. Monitoring changes behaviour even when nobody is watching the data. In my internship
   we had screen-time tracking and people just left a document open in another window. So the
   company got worse data than before, and also lost trust. It was negative on both axes."],
  [*Very strong.* A concrete example, a named second-order effect, and a genuinely new angle.
   Speaking once at 4:00 with this is worth more than speaking five times with Meena's entries.],

  [6:00],
  [*Sneha:* "We have heard a lot from four of us. Meena said something about security earlier that
   we dropped completely, and we have not heard from Nikhil at all. Meena, can you take the security
   side properly? That is the strongest case for monitoring and nobody has made it."],
  [*Top mark on team behaviour.* She named the imbalance, not the person; she *revived a dropped
   point*; she credited Meena; and she invited the silent candidate. Four scores in one sentence.],

  [6:20],
  [*Meena:* "Thank you — yes. The security case is narrow but real: data loss prevention. Not
   watching what someone types, but flagging when 4,000 customer records leave a laptop. That is a
   specific trigger, not surveillance, and I think that is the distinction we have been missing."],
  [*Much stronger than her first entry.* Being invited gave her a shape to fill. Note that Sneha's
   move *created* this contribution.],

  [8:00],
  [*Nikhil:* (first time) "Building on Meena — the distinction is *targeted versus continuous*. A
   trigger on a specific event is defensible. Continuous recording is not. And the test is whether
   the employee can be told about it in advance without the control becoming useless. A data-loss
   trigger still works if everyone knows it exists. Keystroke logging only 'works' if people forget
   it is there — which tells you something."],
  [*Outstanding.* A crisp test that resolves the whole debate. Late but decisive. Timing did not
   hurt him because the content was new.],

  [11:00],
  [*Ravi:* "Shall I summarise? We agreed monitoring is not one thing. We broadly agreed that
   *targeted*, *announced* controls like data-loss triggers are defensible — Meena and Nikhil made
   that case. We split on continuous activity monitoring: Arjun and Karthik argued from the
   employer's right to the time paid for; Sneha and Priya argued it measures the wrong thing and
   degrades the data it collects. We did not discuss the legal position at all, which probably
   differs by country and would change the answer. On balance the group landed on Nikhil's test:
   if a control stops working once you announce it, you should not be doing it."],
  [*The best 40 seconds in the room.* Fair to both sides, names people accurately, adds the missing
   angle, closes with a position rather than a shrug.],
)

#formulas(title: "The ranking the evaluator would write, and why")[
*1. Ravi* — opened with a definition and a structure, separated two conflated arguments mid-way,
and summarised fairly. Note he never "won" anything.

*2. Sneha* — one excellent mechanism-based disagreement, one composed recovery from being
interrupted, and one move that pulled two silent people into the discussion.

*3. Nikhil* — spoke *once*, at minute eight, and resolved the debate. Proof that frequency is not
the metric.

*4. Priya* — one new second-order effect with a concrete example.

*5. Meena* — weak first entry, strong second. Would have ranked higher with Nikhil's discipline of
waiting for something worth saying.

*6. Karthik* — spoke the most and ranked last. Repetition, plus "that is basic".

*Arjun* is unranked in the top six because of the interruption. A single interruption is usually
survivable; combined with an unsupported position, it was not.
]

#trick[
Look at Nikhil again. He spoke *once*, in twelve minutes, at minute eight, and placed third. Copy
that if you are nervous about volume. One genuinely new, well-formed point beats six mediocre ones,
and it is a much easier target to hit when you are anxious.
]

#trap[
Do not copy Nikhil's timing as a *strategy* while having nothing prepared. He placed third because
his point was new and decisive. Staying silent while waiting for a brilliant idea that never arrives
scores zero. If minute six arrives and you have not spoken, use SPEH on the best point you have and
speak.
]

