#section("Part 10 — The 30 minutes before, and the 24 hours after")
// =====================================================================

#table(columns: (auto, 1fr),
  [*When*], [*What to do*],
  [Night before],
  [Re-read the job description and your resume. Write down the three stories you will use. Charge
   everything. Test the video link if it is remote.],
  [30 minutes before],
  [Water within reach, phone silenced but not off, one page of notes: six questions to ask, three
   story titles, your salary floor and target. Do not revise new topics — it raises anxiety and
   changes nothing.],
  [First 60 seconds],
  [Smile, use the interviewer's name once, let them set the pace. If remote, confirm audio in one
   short sentence, then stop talking about the setup.],
  [If the connection drops],
  [Rejoin immediately; if it fails twice, call or message the recruiter within two minutes.
   Recruiters forgive bad networks; they do not forgive a ten-minute silence.],
  [Immediately after],
  [Write down every question you were asked and every one you fumbled. It is the most valuable
   revision document you will ever have, and it evaporates within an hour.],
  [Within 24 hours / after the promised date],
  [Send the thank-you note using the four slots from Part 2. If the decision date passes, wait two
   working days, send one follow-up, wait a week, send one more, then stop — and keep the
   relationship intact, because companies do come back.],
)

#trap[
*Do not send a follow-up every two days.* Two messages over three weeks reads as interest. Six reads
as desperation and gets you filtered.
]

// =====================================================================
#section("Practice")
// =====================================================================

#practice(tier: 1, time: "30 minutes, written")[
1. Write a 30-second GD entry using SPEH on the topic "should engineering colleges make internships
   compulsory?" Label the four slots.
2. Rewrite this email in under 100 words with a searchable subject line: *"Respected Sir, I had
   given my interview last week and I have not heard anything so kindly do the needful and update me
   regarding my status at the earliest as I am very much interested."*
3. You are asked "what is your salary expectation?" in the first HR call for a fresher backend role.
   Write your four-move answer with your own researched numbers.
4. Write the six bond questions as you would actually ask them in a call, in one paragraph.
5. Your notice is 60 days and the company wants you on 1 November. Today is 20 September. Can you
   make it? Show the arithmetic and write the sentence you would say.
6. Write five questions you would ask a hiring manager for a role you actually want. None may be
   answerable from the company website.
]

#key[
*1.* Check your entry has all four: a signpost phrase, exactly one point, evidence that is a number
or a concrete example, and a named handover. If your "evidence" is a restatement of your point,
rewrite it.
*2.* Target shape — Subject: `Following up - <Role> interview, <date> - <Your name>`. Body: name the
recruiter, name the role and date, state the timeline they gave you, add one new piece of
information, one line about timing if you have another process, sign off with your phone number.
Delete every instance of "respected", "kindly", "do the needful", "at the earliest".
*3.* Check: did you ask for their band *first*? Did you give a range with a stated basis? Did you say
the word *fixed*? Did you signal both flexibility and a floor? If any is missing, the answer is not
finished.
*4.* Should contain, in one breath: duration, start point (joining or post-training), exact amount,
whether it reduces pro-rata, whether originals are held, and whether the relieving letter is
withheld. If you did not ask about the relieving letter, you asked the wrong four questions.
*5.* From 20 September, 60 days of notice ends on 19 November, so the last working day is 19
November and you could join 20 November at the earliest. *You cannot make 1 November.* Say: "60 days
from today puts my last working day at 19 November, so 20 November is the date I can commit to. If
my manager waives part of the notice I will tell you immediately, but I do not want to promise
1 November and miss it."
*6.* Each question must fail the website test: if the answer is on the careers page, delete it.
Strong shapes: what the team is building this quarter; the last technical argument and its outcome;
what the team is worst at; what a new joiner should do alone by month six; how the variable
component has actually paid out.
]

#practice(tier: 2, time: "30 minutes")[
1. You have an offer from a Singapore company at SGD 4,500 a month and an Indian offer at Rs 9 LPA
   CTC. List the six things you must know before you can compare them at all.
2. A recruiter in Bangkok asks "why do you want to move to South East Asia?" Write a 45-second
   answer that is not about money and not a platitude.
3. In a GD, one candidate has spoken for four of the first six minutes. Write the exact sentence you
   would use to redirect, without attacking them.
4. You are told the role requires relocating to a city where you know nobody. Write an honest
   answer that neither overpromises nor sounds reluctant.
5. Your visa or work pass timeline is uncertain. Write how you would raise it with the recruiter
   *before* accepting.
6. Write a decline email for an offer you would genuinely have liked, using the four slots.
]

#key[
*1.* (a) Is the SGD figure monthly or annual, and is it 12 or 13 months? (b) What is the fixed
versus variable split in each? (c) Employer CPF or PF contributions, which differ completely between
the two systems. (d) Income tax in each place — the rates and brackets are not comparable. (e) Cost
of living, especially rent, which dominates the Singapore side. (f) Visa or work pass status,
duration, and who pays for it. Only after all six can you compare.
*2.* Strong shapes: a specific product or market you want to work on; regional scale that does not
exist in your current market; a technical problem that is characteristic of that region such as
multi-language or multi-currency systems at scale. Weak shapes: "better opportunities", "exposure",
"growth", "I like travelling".
*3.* Target: *"We have heard a lot of detail from two of us. Meera and Karthik have not come in yet
— can we hear from them before we go further?"* Note that it names the imbalance, not the person,
and it names the people you are inviting.
*4.* Honest shape: say yes clearly if it is yes; name the one practical thing you would need
(notice period, a few weeks to find accommodation); state one true reason you are comfortable with
it. Do not say "I am ready to go anywhere" if you are not — you will be held to it.
*5.* Raise it as a *shared planning problem*, not a confession: "Before I accept, I want us to plan
the pass timeline together. My understanding is it takes X to Y weeks. Can we agree a joining date
that assumes the slower end, so neither of us is surprised?" Never conceal a visa constraint.
*6.* Check: did you decline in the first two lines? Is your reason true and non-comparative? Did you
name something specific about *their* process? Did you leave the door open sincerely?
]

#practice(tier: 3, time: "45 minutes")[
1. You hold two written offers. Offer 1: Rs 11 LPA CTC, of which Rs 2 lakh is variable, no bond,
   90-day notice at the company you would join. Offer 2: Rs 9.5 LPA CTC, all fixed, a Rs 1.5 lakh
   two-year bond, 30-day notice. Compute the monthly in-hand for each using the method in Part 4,
   price the bond per month, and write the two-sentence justification for your choice.
2. Your preferred company has made an offer but is Rs 1.2 lakh below your target. You have one
   competing offer. Write the full counter-offer script using the five slots, then write the reply
   you would send if they say "the base cannot move at all".
3. Write a STAR answer, all four parts labelled, for: "tell me about a time you had to deliver bad
   news to someone senior to you." Use a real situation from your own life.
4. Write a STAR answer for: "tell me about a failure that was genuinely your fault." State what you
   changed afterwards, with evidence.
5. You have a backlog on your transcript. Write the three-sentence honest framing: what happened,
   what you did about it, what the evidence of improvement is. No excuses, no invention.
6. A GD evaluator asks you to summarise a discussion in which your own view was clearly the minority
   one. Write the summary, keeping it fair to the majority.
]

#key[
*1.* Method, not a single right answer. Offer 1's fixed is about Rs 9 lakh before employer PF and
gratuity are stripped out, so its real fixed is lower than Offer 2's headline suggests; run both
through the `inHand` breakdown. The bond on Offer 2 costs $150000/24 = $ Rs 6,250 a month of lost
freedom. The 90-day notice on Offer 1 costs you flexibility *later*, not now. A defensible
justification names the in-hand gap in rupees per month, then the one non-money factor that decided
it. An answer that compares only CTC is wrong regardless of which offer it picks.
*2.* Check the five slots: enthusiasm, the gap as a number, the basis, alternative currencies,
a precise close. For the "base cannot move" reply, the strong shape pivots immediately to the
alternatives you already named — joining bonus, six-month review, relocation, title, a shorter bond
— and still ends with a commitment: "if we can do X, I will accept today."
*3.* Check: does *T* say what was at risk *for you*? Does *A* show you told them *early* rather than
hoping it would resolve? Does *A* include what you brought *besides* the problem — an option, a
mitigation, a plan? Does *R* include the senior person's reaction, not just the outcome?
*4.* Check: is the failure genuinely yours, and not a disguised strength ("I worked too hard")? Does
*A* include what you did in the moment, not only afterwards? Does *R* include a *changed behaviour*
with evidence that it stuck — a checklist you still use, a test you now always write, a habit
someone else noticed?
*5.* Target shape: *"I had a backlog in <subject> in my fourth semester. I had taken on too much
outside coursework and I misjudged how far behind I was until it was too late. I cleared it in the
next attempt with <grade>, and my CGPA rose from X to Y over the following three semesters."* Three
sentences: fact, cause owned, evidence. No blame on the college, the teacher, or illness unless it
is true and you are comfortable stating it. Then stop talking and let them move on.
*6.* Check: did you state the majority view *first* and *accurately*? Did you avoid using the
summary to re-argue your own position? A fair summary that clearly includes the view you disagreed
with is one of the strongest signals available in a GD.
]

#revision[
*GD — the grid.* Content · Structure · Initiative · Team behaviour · Clarity · Listening.
Aggression is a *negative* mark. Four to six entries of 25 to 40 seconds is the target.

*GD — every entry is SPEH.* Signpost $->$ Point $->$ Evidence $->$ Handover by name. The handover is
the slot everyone drops and the one that scores.

*GD — the four hard moments.* Talked over: finish calmly afterwards. Dominator: name the
*imbalance*, not the person. Wrong fact: correct the *claim*, not the person. Stalled: summarise and
open a new angle.

*GD — the summary.* Agreements $->$ disagreements, fairly $->$ what we missed $->$ a position.
Never invent a consensus.

*Email — four rules.* Subject carries the fact · first sentence says the ask · under 150 words ·
one ask per message. Delete "do the needful", "esteemed organisation", "at the earliest", "revert
back".

*Questions to ask.* Prepare six, ask two or three. Ask about *the work*, not the benefits. Best
single question: "what is the team worst at right now?"

*Money — the one rule.* CTC is not salary. Strip out variable, joining bonus, employer PF, gratuity
and insurance. What is left is fixed pay; subtract your PF and tax to get in-hand. A *lower* CTC
can pay *more* every month.

*Salary answer — four moves.* Ask for their band $->$ give a researched range, not a point $->$ say
the word *fixed* $->$ signal flexibility *and* a floor.

*Counter-offer — five slots.* Enthusiasm $->$ the gap as a number $->$ the basis $->$ alternative
currencies (joining bonus, early review, relocation, shorter bond) $->$ a precise close: "if we get
to X, I accept today."

*Bond — six questions.* Duration · amount · pro-rata or not · trigger · original documents ·
*relieving letter*. The last one is the one that can block your next job.

*Notice period.* Plan backwards from the joining date. Commit to *one* date you can actually meet.
Buy-out and manager waivers are *upside*, never promises. Never resign before the written offer is
in hand.

*STAR, always all four parts.* Situation, Task, Action, Result — and the Result must be measurable.
Use *your own* experience. Never invent one.

*Honest framing of a weakness.* One sentence of fact, one of ownership, one of evidence that it
improved. Then stop.

*After the interview.* Write down every question within the hour. Thank-you note within 24 hours.
Follow up twice over three weeks, then stop.
